# Vauxhall Cavalier Mk2 four-door saloon, 1981-1988.
#
# The front-wheel-drive Cavalier: GM's J-car, the same car as the Opel Ascona C,
# built at Luton from 17 August 1981 and launched to the public on 23 September
# 1981. Two- and four-door saloons and a five-door hatchback; the two-door was
# dropped in the UK after October 1982, and an estate joined in October 1983.
# Facelifts in 1985 (egg-crate grille, darker rear lamps) and 1987 (body-colour
# grille, revised lamps). Britain's second best-selling car in 1984 and 1985.
# https://www.aronline.co.uk/cars/vauxhall/cavalier/the-cars-vauxhall-cavalier-mk2/
# http://cavalierandchevetteclub.com/cavalier%20mk2%20saloon%20and%20hatch.htm
#
# Dimensions: 4,320 mm long (saloon), 1,668 mm wide, 1,390 mm high, wheelbase
# 2,574 mm.
# https://www.motor-car.net/vauxhall/item/20845-opel-ascona-c
#
# Colours: Carmine Red, Astro Silver, White Gold and Helios Blue are documented
# Vauxhall colours on sale during the Mk2's life (Astro Silver 1978-90, White
# Gold 1978-87, Carmine Red 1980-87, Helios Blue 1982-88).
# https://www.bentasker.co.uk/images/Documentation/Vauxhall_Paint_Codes_-_Car_Touch_Up_Paint_-_Car_Paint.pdf
#
# Colour shares and weight: the Mk2 was a top-two UK seller at its peak, so it is
# set at 300 against the Escort's 500 anchor, split by the measured 1980s share
# order: blue 95, red 75, silver 70, white 60. Provisional pending the central
# rebase.
#
# Speed 160 km/h: a game-balance figure for the 1.3-2.0 range.
# Comfort 100/480: a 1980s family saloon, above the Mk1 Cavalier's 85/458 and
# below the Sierra's 120/510; the line gives 330.
#
# Dates describe road presence: launch September 1981 with a 12-14 year tail,
# staggered by colour.
#
# Metres master: citycars/4.5/vauxhall-cavalier-mk2.blend in the blends
# repository. References: citycars/references/vauxhall-cavalier-mk2/ (gitignored).
# Image credits: 1986 SRi and 1984 GL photographs via Wikimedia Commons
# (CC BY-SA 2.0); see the reference file names.

